EXT. MUDDY TRENCH - DAWN

A cold morning. Rain-fed mud, duckboards, a sagging tarp strung overhead. Smoke from a tin stove.

SERGEANT ABEL TOROSYAN (40s, helmet, gray in his stubble) hunches over a mess tin. Beside him, PRIVATE LUCIEN MARCHAND (20s, bandaged ear) peels a potato. CORPORAL HAYDEN BRIGGS (30s, tall, tobacco-stained moustache) cleans his rifle. PRIVATE FELICITY "FLIP" OKONKWO (20s, medic's armband) rewinds a bandage roll. PRIVATE EAMON DOHERTY (19, scrawny) writes a letter on his knee. And young LIEUTENANT SASHA VOLKOV (20s, polished boots now caked in mud) studies a map.

TOROSYAN
Anyone hear the guns?

BRIGGS
Not since four.

LUCIEN
They're resting.

FLIP
Everyone rests.

VOLKOV
(to the trench)
Gentlemen, a reminder. We move at noon.

EAMON
(muttering)
Noon. Always noon.

A shell whistles overhead, high and wide, and bursts far behind the line. Every man ducks. Lucien drops the potato. Briggs sweeps the rifle against his chest. Flip falls flat. Eamon covers his letter with his body. Torosyan, last, tucks his tin under his arm.

Silence. Then Briggs laughs, once, and the others join, a dry reluctant sound.

TOROSYAN
Everyone alive?

LUCIEN
My potato's dead.

FLIP
Pity.
